<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">COMPARE / THE ACTUAL DIFFERENCES</span><h1>Your next edge.<br><em>Side by side.</em></h1><p class="lede">Compare the operational model as well as the packet path. See what changes when you choose a connected software platform, another software vendor, a dedicated appliance or your own stack.</p></section><nav class="page-jumps" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#matrix">Comparison matrix ↓</a><a href="#tradeoffs">Who each approach suits ↓</a><a href="#costs">Cost worksheet ↓</a></nav><section class="section" id="matrix"><span class="eyebrow">12 AREAS / FOUR APPROACHES</span><h2>What are you really choosing?</h2><p class="status-note"><strong>Read the status, not just the tick.</strong> LiquidNAT’s public Manager is an interactive simulation. Planned platform functions and independently tested engine foundations are identified separately. This table does not claim shipping feature parity.</p><div class="comparison-controls" role="group" aria-label="Comparison focus"><button class="active" data-focus="all" aria-pressed="true">The whole picture</button><button data-focus="operations" aria-pressed="false">Operations</button><button data-focus="economics" aria-pressed="false">Economics</button><button data-focus="migration" aria-pressed="false">Migration</button></div><p id="filter-status" class="fine" role="status">Showing all 12 evaluation areas.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Platform approaches: concrete differences and implementation boundaries"><table><caption>Platform approaches: concrete differences and implementation boundaries</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Compare</th><th scope="col">LiquidNAT</th><th scope="col">netElastic</th><th scope="col">Dedicated appliance</th><th scope="col">Assembled software stack</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-category="economics"><th scope="row">Hardware model</th><td>Own-server architecture; compatibility and capacity still need validation.</td><td>Software on x86; packaged appliance options also offered.</td><td>Typically a vendor platform with a defined hardware lifecycle.</td><td>Select Linux servers, NICs and components yourself.</td></tr><tr data-category="operations"><th scope="row">Subscriber edge</th><td>BNG design spans PPPoE/IPoE. The browser demonstrates subscriber workflows; live BNG is not yet proven.</td><td>vBNG lists PPPoE, IPoE and RADIUS AAA with CoA.</td><td>Choose a specific BNG-capable model and software licence.</td><td>Build access termination, identity and policy integration.</td></tr><tr data-category="operations"><th scope="row">CGNAT</th><td>Working demo for mode changes, pool admission and tuple/time lookup. Packet-engine NAT remains implementation work.</td><td>Separate CGNAT product, with optional integrated CGNAT in BNG packages.</td><td>Check whether NAT is on-box, licensed separately or a separate system.</td><td>Combine a NAT implementation with allocation, retention and lookup tools.</td></tr><tr data-category="operations"><th scope="row">Direct & static IPv4</th><td>Demo switches individual subscribers between CGNAT, direct and reserved static addresses.</td><td>Confirm per-service addressing and migration behaviour for the proposed release.</td><td>Review subscriber addressing and public-prefix ownership.</td><td>Address ownership and policy reconciliation are your integration work.</td></tr><tr data-category="operations"><th scope="row">IPv6</th><td>Demo separates WAN IPv6 and delegated prefixes; live subscriber IPv6 needs validation.</td><td>BNG packages list IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack routing.</td><td>Verify exact access and prefix-delegation combination.</td><td>Integrate delegation, neighbour discovery, routing and policy.</td></tr><tr data-category="operations"><th scope="row">Operator interface</th><td>Manager demo links search, service profiles, diagnostics and audit history in one workspace.</td><td>netVision provides router management and troubleshooting.</td><td>Compare the vendor management suite and subscriber visibility.</td><td>Assemble dashboards, identity correlation and operational tooling.</td></tr><tr data-category="operations"><th scope="row">Policy changes</th><td>Custom demo speed profiles, review/apply and subscriber assignment. Actual shaping and CoA are not yet connected.</td><td>Published BNG features include RADIUS rate plans and QoS.</td><td>Verify live changes, reconnect impact and rollback semantics.</td><td>Own the policy schema and safe application across components.</td></tr><tr data-category="operations"><th scope="row">Failure handling</th><td>Demo jobs illustrate failover and rollback; they are not evidence of flow continuity.</td><td>Request release- and topology-specific failure-test results.</td><td>Validate each failure domain; redundancy alone does not establish session survival.</td><td>Test the whole service, not just each component restarting.</td></tr><tr data-category="economics"><th scope="row">Commercial comparison</th><td>Request a scoped proposal; the site does not publish licence prices or savings claims.</td><td>Request the relevant software/appliance, capacity and support quote.</td><td>Include capacity licences, hardware refresh and support renewals.</td><td>Include engineering, integration, testing and on-call ownership.</td></tr><tr data-category="economics"><th scope="row">Capacity evidence</th><td>Demo counters are simulated. Independent engine forwarding tests are narrower than a full BNG/CGNAT benchmark.</td><td>Compare a specific package and enabled-feature test profile.</td><td>Use the actual line cards, enabled services and redundancy reserve.</td><td>Reproduce a representative workload on the exact assembled versions.</td></tr><tr data-category="migration"><th scope="row">Integration ownership</th><td>A connected product-family design; production billing/RADIUS adapters need agreed scope.</td><td>Check supported APIs and AAA integration for your estate.</td><td>Assess supported provisioning systems and management APIs.</td><td>Your team owns lifecycle retries, reconciliation and version compatibility.</td></tr><tr data-category="migration"><th scope="row">Best starting point</th><td>Hands-on workflow evaluation and a scoped technical discussion.</td><td>A shortlist requiring a quoted commercial vBNG/CGNAT product.</td><td>An estate prioritising a vendor hardware ecosystem.</td><td>A team resourced to build and maintain its own platform.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="fine">netElastic positioning and listed capabilities: <a href="https://netelastic.com/products" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">official products</a> and <a href="https://netelastic.com/products/packages/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">package specifications</a>, reviewed 5 November 2026. Appliance and assembled-stack columns describe common procurement models, not every vendor. Exact release, licence and topology matter.</p></section><section class="section" id="tradeoffs"><span class="eyebrow">MATCH THE OPERATING MODEL</span><h2>Different strengths.<br>Different responsibilities.</h2><div class="cards"><article><h3>LiquidNAT</h3><p>Choose it for a hands-on exploration of connected subscriber operations and a discussion about the platform being built. Bring a representative use case; establish release scope and acceptance evidence before planning a production cutover.</p></article><article><h3>Commercial software edge</h3><p>Suitable when server choice and an established supplier’s product scope are priorities. Compare software-only and appliance offers, hardware qualification, support boundaries and capacity entitlements together.</p></article><article><h3>Dedicated appliance estate</h3><p>Suitable when standardising on an existing vendor, sparing model and support organisation matters most. Include the refresh cycle, expansion steps and subscriber tooling in the decision.</p></article><article><h3>Build your own</h3><p>Suitable when deep customisation is worth owning the integration. Budget for upgrades, failure tests, documentation, security response and engineers who can support the complete service.</p></article></div></section><section class="section" id="costs"><span class="eyebrow">YOUR INPUTS / NO ASSUMED PRICES</span><h2>Compare the whole cost.</h2><p>Enter like-for-like quotes in your own currency. The worksheet adds upfront costs and recurring costs across your planning horizon. Values stay in this browser page and are not sent or saved.</p><label class="horizon">Planning horizon (years)<input id="cost-years" type="number" value="3" min="1" max="10" step="1"></label><div class="cost-grid"><fieldset class="cost-plan" data-plan="0"><legend>LiquidNAT proposal</legend><label>Hardware & initial software<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="hardware"></label><label>Migration & engineering<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="migration"></label><label>Annual licences & support<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="annual"></label><label>Monthly power, hosting & operations<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="monthly"></label><output id="cost-total-0">Enter your costs</output></fieldset><fieldset class="cost-plan" data-plan="1"><legend>Alternative proposal</legend><label>Hardware & initial software<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="hardware"></label><label>Migration & engineering<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="migration"></label><label>Annual licences & support<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="annual"></label><label>Monthly power, hosting & operations<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="monthly"></label><output id="cost-total-1">Enter your costs</output></fieldset><fieldset class="cost-plan" data-plan="2"><legend>Current / assembled stack</legend><label>Hardware & initial software<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="hardware"></label><label>Migration & engineering<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="migration"></label><label>Annual licences & support<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="annual"></label><label>Monthly power, hosting & operations<input type="number" min="0" max="1000000000" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" value="0" data-cost="monthly"></label><output id="cost-total-2">Enter your costs</output></fieldset></div><p id="cost-status" role="status" class="fine">Total = hardware + migration + years × annual + years × 12 × monthly. This is a planning worksheet, not a quote.</p><button type="button" class="button" id="export-costs">Download comparison CSV ↓</button></section><section class="section"><a class="comparison-link" href="compare-netelastic.php"><div><span class="eyebrow">THE NAMED COMPARISON</span><h3>LiquidNAT vs netElastic.</h3><p>Product scope, operations and the differences to validate in a technical evaluation.</p></div><span>↗</span></a></section>
